e_margin": -5.28, "analyst_adjustment": 0.28, "key_factors": ["District's strong Republican lean (R+9 PVI) anchors the race", "Laubacher's massive fundraising edge ($10.3M vs $1.0M) keeps her competitive", "Mixed expert ratings: mostly Likely/Solid R, but three raters call it a Tossup", "Single September poll shows Boebert up only 5 in a Dem-sponsored survey"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican hold in an R+9 district with an incumbent, but Laubacher's extraordinary fundraising and a handful of Tossup ratings from respected forecasters suggest the race is tighter than the district's lean implies. A margin near R+5 balances the partisan baseline against her financial advantage.", "watch": "Any late independent polling or a shift in expert ratings after the October debate could move this toward Tossup or back toward Likely R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Lauren Boebert (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 4th District pairs a strongly Republican-leaning electorate with an unusually well-funded challenger. Incumbent Lauren Boebert carries the advantages of incumbency and partisanship in a district Trump-friendly territory, but Democrat Eileen Laubacher has raised over $10 million to Boebert's roughly $1 million, giving her the resources to compete on the airwaves. Expert forecasters mostly rate the race Likely or Solid Republican, though three outlets call it a Tossup, and a September poll showed Boebert leading by just five points. Prediction markets give Republicans roughly a 70 percent chance. The race drew wide attention with a single televised debate in mid-October covering tariffs, immigration, and healthcare. The forecast expects Boebert to win by a modest margin, with meaningful uncertainty given thin polling and the challenger's financial strength.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1824, "margin": -5.77}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3343, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1945, "m